Key C is the intended and normally-correct answer, but both the stem and the explanation state the rule more broadly than it holds. The explanation says "Since C++11 a destructor with no explicit exception specification is implicitly noexcept". That is not unconditional: a destructor with no exception-specification of its own gets an IMPLICIT exception specification computed from its bases and members, and it is potentially-throwing if any base or member destructor is potentially-throwing ([except.spec]). The stem's condition — "a destructor that carries no exception specification of its own" — is precisely the condition my counterexample satisfies, so as written the stem admits option A as a correct answer for a real class. Verified by compiling and running (g++ 13.3, -std=c++17):

  struct Thrower { ~Thrower() noexcept(false) {} };
  struct HasThrowingMember { Thrower t; ~HasThrowingMember() { throw 2; } };
  try { HasThrowingMember h; } catch (int e) { printf("CAUGHT %d\n", e); }

It printed `HasThrowingMember dtor noexcept? 0` and `CAUGHT 2 from a dtor with no exception-spec of its own` — i.e. the exception propagated and was caught by an enclosing handler, which is option A, not option C. (The control case `struct Plain { ~Plain() { throw 1; } };` reported noexcept? 1 and did call std::terminate, confirming C for the default case.) Fix by pinning the stem to a class with no throwing subobjects and by qualifying the rule in the explanation.
